.TH SECURITY-LOGIN 2
.SH NAME
login \- verify credentials
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "keyring.m";
include "security.m";
login := load Login Login->PATH;

login: fn(name, password, addr: string):
	(string, ref Keyring->Authinfo);

okname:  fn(name: string): string;
cnsamode: fn(): int;
cnsaalg:  fn(alg: string): string;
cnsahash: fn(ha: string): string;
cnsakey:  fn(info: ref Keyring->Authinfo): string;
putstring: fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, s: string): string;
readsecret: fn(io: ref Sys->FD): (array of byte, string);
secret:   fn(name: string, password: array of byte): array of byte;
oldsecret: fn(password: array of byte): array of byte;
secretform: fn(s: array of byte): int;
zero:     fn(a: array of byte);
runes:    fn(a: array of byte): int;
charge:  fn(prog, name: string): (int, string);
cleared: fn(prog, name: string);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR Login
module is provided for use by a client of
a certifying authority (CA) or `signer'.
The
.B login
function communicates
with a certifying authority (CA)
in order to create a
.B Keyring->Authinfo
adt
which contains a public/private key pair and a certificate
signed by the CA
(see
.IR keyring-intro (2)).
The public/private key pair is generated by
.B login
using the same parameters as those in the signer's key
(eg, algorithm and key length);
see
.IR keyring-gensk (2).
The procedure assumes a secret, i.e. a
password, has already been established
between the user and the CA.
See
.IR changelogin (8)
and
.IR keyfs (4)
for how this password is managed at the
CA.
.PP
.B Login
connects, using
.IR dial (2),
to the signer at network address
.IR addr ,
which is any form accepted by
.IR cs (8),
including the special address
.BR $SIGNER ,
which
.IR cs
will translate to the client's default signer (if there is one).
Normally the incoming call will be given to
.IR logind (8)
by
.IR svc (8).
.PP
.B Login
sends the user
.I name
and
.IR password ,
using the protocol described in
.IR login (6),
to justify the server's
issuing a certificate, which is returned in a
.B Keyring->Authinfo
adt on success.
The certificate can if desired be stored by
.BR Keyring->writeauthinfo ;
see
.IR keyring-auth (2).
The password is used by the encrypted
key exchange protocol to establish
a secure channel between user and CA.
.PP
.B Login
returns only when the signer has proved that it has the password too,
and what it returns it has checked:
the signer's public key and Diffie-Hellman parameters
.RB ( alpha
and
.BR p ,
which
.IR security-auth (2)
uses) come under the session's keys from that signer, and the certificate
verifies with the signer's key for the key
.B login
made.
The exchange's own Diffie-Hellman group is one of the three in
.IR login (6),
which the module holds; nothing is taken from the signer on trust.
In CNSA mode
.RB ( /env/cnsamode
set)
.B login
offers only the CNSA suite and refuses a group of fewer than 3072 bits,
a signer whose key is not ML-DSA-87, a signer's modulus of fewer than 3072 bits,
and a certificate that is not ML-DSA-87's under SHA-384 or SHA-512.
.PP
.B Cnsamode
reports whether CNSA 2.0 mode is on
.RB ( /env/cnsamode
holding anything that does not begin with
.BR 0 ,
.B n
or
.BR N ).
The mode is a rule for every key made or taken in it, and these state the rule once:
.B cnsaalg
returns nil for the one signature algorithm it takes
.RB ( mldsa87 ,
.BR Login->CNSAsig )
and otherwise the reason;
.B cnsahash
the same for a certificate's hash
.RB ( sha384
or
.BR sha512 );
.B cnsakey
the same for a whole key file as
.B Keyring->readauthinfo
gives it: its keys, its signer's key, its certificate's algorithm and hash, and
its Diffie-Hellman modulus, which must have
.B Login->CNSAdhbits
(3072) bits or more.
.B Putstring
sends
.I s
as one of Keyring's messages (see
.IR keyring-getstring (2))
and returns nil, or sends nothing and returns the reason when
.I s
does not fit one:
.B Keyring->putstring
cuts such a string short and reports success.
.PP
.B Readsecret
reads a line typed on
.I io
with the console's echo off (it writes
.B rawon
and then
.B rawoff
to
.BR /dev/consctl )
and returns its bytes, without the newline, or nil and the reason.
In raw mode the console edits nothing, so
.B readsecret
does: a backspace or delete removes the character before it,
control-U the line so far, and a carriage return is dropped.
The secret is never a string, which a Limbo program cannot clear;
the caller zeroes the array with
.B zero
when it is done.
.B Runes
counts the characters of UTF-8 in an array.
.PP
.B Secret
makes the secret
.IR keyfs (4)
holds for the account
.I name
with
.I password
.RI ( keys (6)):
48 bytes
.RB ( Login->Secretlen )
of PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA-384,
.B Login->Secretiter
(200000) iterations, salted with
.B "infernode login secret 2"
a zero byte and
.IR name .
.B Oldsecret
makes the old form, the password's SHA-256 (32 bytes,
.BR Login->Oldsecretlen ).
.B Secretform
says by its length which a stored secret is:
.BR Login->Newform ,
.BR Login->Oldform ,
or 0, neither.
.PP
.B Login
itself zeroes the password's bytes and secret, the shared secret's bytes
and the session's keys before it returns;
the password it is given is a string, and stays the caller's.
.PP
The other functions are for the signer's programs, which share them so
that there is one of each.
.B Okname
returns nil if
.I name
is one account name as
.IR login (6)
defines it, and otherwise the reason it is not;
.BR login ,
.IR logind (8),
.IR keysrv (4),
.IR signer (8)
and
.IR changelogin (8)
refuse a name it refuses.
.B Charge
and
.B cleared
are the count of failed attempts described in
.IR logind (8),
kept in
.BR /keydb/loginfail :
.B charge
counts an attempt for
.I name
and returns 0, or returns the seconds for which
.I name
is still refused (and counts nothing), or an error string if the count cannot be kept;
.B cleared
removes the count once the password has been proved.
.I Prog
is the caller's name for the console and the audit log.
The module also exports the pieces of the exchange both ends compute
.RB ( group ,
.BR exponent ,
.BR mask ,
.BR keys
and the rest; see
.B security.m
and
.IR login (6)).
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/login.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR getauthinfo (8),
.IR keyring-auth (2),
.IR login (6),
.IR createsignerkey (8),
.IR logind (8)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B Login
returns nil in the string component
on success and a diagnostic string on error (with a nil
.B Keyring->Authinfo
reference).
